# Design Patterns (Mandatory)

Use the right pattern for the job. Do not force patterns on trivial code.

## Required patterns in this project

| Pattern | Use when | Location |
|---------|----------|----------|
| **MVC** | Every HTTP feature | Route + Controller + Inertia Page |
| **Form Request** | Any validated input | `Http/Requests/` |
| **Action** | Single write operation (create, update, delete) | `Actions/` |
| **Service** | Multi-step workflow or read orchestration | `Services/` |
| **Repository** | Complex/reused queries (3+ call sites) | `Repositories/` + interface |
| **DTO** | Structured data between layers | `Data/` or readonly PHP class |
| **Factory** | Test data and seeders | `database/factories/` |
| **Observer / Event** | Side effects after model change (audit, notify) | `Observers/`, `Events/` |
| **Policy** | Authorization rules | `Policies/` |
| **Presentational / Container** | Smart page vs dumb component | `pages/` vs `components/` |

## Design principles

- **Separation of Concerns**: Each file one job.
- **KISS**: Simplest solution that meets requirements.
- **YAGNI**: No speculative abstractions.
- **Composition over inheritance**: Prefer traits/concerns and injection.
- **Convention over configuration**: Follow Laravel + Inertia conventions first.

## Anti-patterns (never use)

- God Controller / God Model / God Component
- Business logic in Blade, React page, or Migration
- Direct `DB::` in Controller when Eloquent/Repository suffices
- Copy-paste validation (backend + frontend must both validate, but rules defined once on server)
